  "statement": "HR stores the reporting chain as a binary tree and wants to know how many levels it has.\n\nGiven the `root` of a binary tree, return its **maximum depth**: the number of nodes on the longest path from the root down to a leaf. An empty tree has depth 0.\n\nTrees are passed as a `TreeNode` (with `.val`, `.left`, `.right`; the class is already defined for you). In examples a tree is written in level order with `null` for missing children, for example `[3, 9, 20, null, null, 15, 7]`, and `[]` means an empty tree (`None`).",
  "function": {
    "name": "max_depth",
    "params": [
      {
        "name": "root",
        "type": "TreeNode"
      }
    ],
    "returns": "int"
  },
  "constraints": [
    "0 <= number of nodes <= 10^4"
  ],
  "examples": [
    {
      "args": [
        [
          3,
          9,
          20,
          null,
          null,
          15,
          7
        ]
      ],
      "expected": 3,
      "explanation": "The path 3 -> 20 -> 15 has three nodes."
    },
    {
      "args": [
        []
      ],
      "expected": 0,
      "explanation": "An empty tree has no levels."
    }
  ],
